[{:db/id -1 :doc/id "business-openrouter-fleet-primary-modal-backup-20260908" :doc/title "Murakumo fleet primary, Modal backup" :doc/as-of "2026-09-08" :doc/status :accepted :source/dataset "business" :doc/body "# Murakumo fleet primary; Modal stability backup\n\nOwner clarification, 2026-09-08: Murakumo fleet is the primary compute service. Modal is a backup for stability; failback returns new traffic to the fleet after verified recovery. Slow, but cheap remains the product concept. The agent's previous Modal-primary interpretation and resulting pause of fleet qualification were incorrect.\n\nQualification continues on the existing fixed fleet candidate. Preserve raw b70 observations, including the pause interval; resume the existing 15-minute observation automation. Do not reset the start date, backfill the gap, or count Modal completions as fleet successes. Report fleet-only success/capacity and overall service availability separately.\n\nFailover is a bounded reliability mechanism, not a latency optimizer: a slower healthy fleet response alone is not a trigger. Candidate triggers are measured unavailability, capacity exhaustion or a deadline that cannot be met. Require a verified compatible model, context, supported parameters and declared retention policy. Current b70 Qwen3.8-27B and the configured Modal GLM-5.3-Flash are different models and cannot substitute for each other under one fixed OpenRouter model identity. A matching Modal deployment or separately advertised model is required; do not silently alias across them.\n\nFailback requires successful fleet probes and capacity recovery, with a cooldown to avoid oscillation. Route new requests to fleet; do not migrate or restart a partially emitted stream behind the user's back. Preserve cancellation and request/attempt identifiers across the routing decision. Explicit OpenRouter offers must not fall back to OpenRouter itself.\n\nEconomics: price from fleet costs plus measured backup frequency and backup costs, retries, idle/reserved capacity, edge overhead and partner charges. Modal Shared list rates are a backup cost input, not the cost of every fleet request. The preceding comparison between Modal GLM prices and the b70 Qwen selling price did not establish that the fleet offer is loss-making. It compared distinct backends/models. Enforce a backup spend limit and define the response when it is exhausted.\n\nData policy must disclose both the primary path and conditional backup processing. Modal contract/account work remains necessary before activating that leg for partner traffic, but is not a blocker to fleet sustained-load, cancellation or recovery testing. The offline invoice reconciliation tool remains useful for backup vendor costs; it does not implement production partner billing.\n\nThis record corrects the architecture and qualification plan. It does not claim production automatic failover/failback or a matching Modal backup is implemented or verified. The live routing configuration is unchanged by this documentation correction.\n"}]
